    "summary": "Eyes Of The World jam shines; solid set with strong vocal moments",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a standout Eyes Of The World jam that flows from Truckin', followed by a heartfelt Stella Blue. Jerry’s guitar stays on point while Phil adds an expressive bass solo, and the band rides the relaxed vibe set by Donna’s vocals.",
    "review": "Feb 21, 1973 at Assembly Hall captured one of the early‑’70s Dead in fine form. Set 1 rolls through a tight mix of Americana and rock – from the crisp “Brown Eyed Women” to the soulful “Looks Like Rain,” with Donna Jean’s vocals adding extra color. The second set is where the magic kicks in: a loose Truckin' jam that morphs into an exhilarating Eyes Of The World, capped by a plaintive Stella Blue before the band closes out with Sugar Magnolia and Casey Jones. Jerry delivers a concise but tasteful solo after the Eyes transition, Phil shines with an expressive bass solo during that same jam, and Bob’s on‑stage banter (“Rockin’ your socks off”) keeps the energy light. The rhythm section – Bill on drums – provides a steady groove that lets the improvisations breathe. While the Miller soundboard is generally crisp, occasional drop‑outs are noted; nevertheless it remains the cleanest source for this performance.",
